# Fine-Tuning of Molecular Packing and Energy Level through Methyl Substitution Enabling Excellent Small Molecule Acceptors for Nonfullerene Polymer Solar Cells with Efficiency up to 12.54%

## abstract
A novel small molecule acceptor MeIC with a methylated end-capping group is developed. Compared to unmethylated counterparts (ITCPTC), MeIC exhibits a higher lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level value, tighter molecular packing, better crystallites quality, and stronger absorption in the range of 520-740 nm. The MeIC-based polymer solar cells (PSCs) with J71 as donor, achieve high power conversion efficiency (PCE), up to 12.54% with a short-circuit current (JSC ) of 18.41 mA cm-2 , significantly higher than that of the device based on J71:ITCPTC (11.63% with a JSC of 17.52 mA cm-2 ). The higher JSC of the PSC based on J71:MeIC can be attributed to more balanced μh /μe , higher charge dissociation and charge collection efficiency, better molecular packing, and more proper phase separation features as indicated by grazing incident X-ray diffraction and resonant soft X-ray scattering results. It is worth mentioning that the as-cast PSCs based on MeIC also yield a high PCE of 11.26%, which is among the highest value for the as-cast nonfullerene PSCs so far. Such a small modification that leads to so significant an improvement of the photovoltaic performance is a quite exciting finding, shining a light on the molecular design of the nonfullerene acceptors.

A novel small molecule acceptor MeIC with a methylated end-capping group is developed. Compared to unmethylated counterparts (ITCPTC), MeIC exhibits a higher lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level value, tighter molecular packing, better crystallites quality, and stronger absorption in the range of $520\mathrm{-}740\mathrm{nm}$ . The MeIC-based polymer solar cells (PSCs) with J71 as donor, achieve high power conversion efficiency (PCE), up to $12.54\%$ with a short-circuit current $(\boldsymbol{J}_{s c})$ of $\mathsf{18.47\ m A c m^{-2}}$ , significantly higher than that of the device based on J71:ITCPTC ( $11.63\%$ with a $J_{s c}$ of $\mathsf{17.52}\mathsf{m A c m^{-2}})$ . The higher $J_{s c}$ of the PSC based on J71:MeIC can be attributed to more balanced $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}/\mu_{\mathrm{e}},$ higher charge dissociation and charge collection efficiency, better molecular packing, and more proper phase separation features as indicated by grazing incident X-ray diffraction and resonant soft X-ray scattering results. It is worth mentioning that the as-cast PSCs based on MeIC also yield a high PCE of $11.26\%$ , which is among the highest value for the as-cast nonfullerene PSCs so far. Such a small modification that leads to so significant an improvement of the photovoltaic performance is a quite exciting finding, shining a light on the molecular design of the nonfullerene acceptors.
Polymer solar cells (PSCs) are of multiple advantages such as low cost, light weight, low temperature printing fabrication and flexibility.[1–14] In recent years, series of nonfullerene electron acceptor materials including small molecules and polymers have been developed to match with the highly efficient polymer donors.[15–21] And the devices based on the small molecular acceptors (SMAs) exhibited power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) over $12\%$ .[22–24] SMAs have shown promising potential due to their strong light absorption, tunable electronic energy levels and range of absorption.[25–27] In general, the chemical structures of the SMAs contain different electron-withdrawing building blocks, such as imide,[28–35] rhodamine,[36,37] benzothiadiazole,[38] and 2-(3-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-1-ylidene)malononitrile (INCN).[39–46]Amongthem,INCNsystemhasbeen demonstrated to be the most successful.[24]
The rational design of SMAs is typically based on crystal engineering and molecular orbital energetics strategies that are employed to effectively mediate the $\pi$ -orbital overlap and frontier orbital energy levels.[47–50] Several design methods have been exploited to alter the packing of
SMAs, including the introduction of $\mathrm{~F~}$ atom, and substitution with different side chains.[23,39,46] For example, Zhan and coworkers have reported three nonfullerene acceptors, ITIC, ITICTh, and ITIC-Th1 with the end-capping group INCN.[23,39,40]
From ITIC to ITIC-Th, the change of the side chains from 4-hexylphenyl to 5-hexylthienyl enhances the intermolecular interaction, which improve $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and charge transport. Compared with ITIC-Th, fluorinated electron acceptor of ITIC-Th1 showed a redshifted absorption and better intramole­ cular interaction through CF⋯S, CF⋯H, and $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{F}{\cdots}{\pi}$ noncovalent interactions, and thus higher short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ and PCE were obtained; Li and coworkers reported that the side chain isomerization of the alkyl substituent group of ITIC from 4-hexylphenyl to 3-hexylphenyl resulted in more evident crystallinity and higher electron mobility compared with ITIC, and consequently a higher fill factor (FF) and PCE were achieved.[47] What is more, finely tuning the terminal acceptor unit to increase the local intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction is an effective method to improve the device performance.[50,51] We recently developed a novel electron acceptor (ITCPTC) based on a seven-ring fused core and thiophene-fused ending group, namely CPTCN. Compared with the classic acceptor of ITIC with a benzene-fused end-capping group, the thiophene-fused ending group can promote material interactions and crystallization. As a result, using the wide-bandgap polymer PBT1-EH as the donor and ITCPTC as the acceptor, PCEs of up to $11.8\%$ was achieved, with an enhanced $J_{\mathrm{SC}},$ but a declined open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ .[52]
To further boost the device performance, herein we designed and synthesized a new small molecule acceptor, namely MeIC, by introducing methyl onto the thiophene of the ending group on the basis of ITCPTC (Figure 1a). On the one hand, methyl is a weak electron-donating unit, which can upshift lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels to increase the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . On the other hand, the introduction of methyl will not lead to bulky 3D conformation as in the case of long alkyl substitution, therefore molecular packing will be less disturbed. Moreover, we anticipate that the small modification could fine tune the intramolecular interaction and crystallization. The PSCs based on the new MeIC acceptor and J71 polymer donor (Figure 1a) showed high PCEs of up to $12.54\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.918\mathrm{~V~},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $18.41\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and FF of $74.2\%$ , which are significantly higher compared to those based on ITCPTC analog $(11.63\%)$ . It is also worth mentioning that the PSCs based on MeIC without extra treatment also yield high PCE of $11.26\%$ , which is very conducive to low-cost industry-scale production of the PSCs. To the best of our knowledge, $11.26\%$ is one of the highest PCE for the as-cast nonfullerene PSCs so far. Such a small modification that lead to the significant improvement of the photovoltaic performance is a quite exciting finding, which represents a simple methodology to boost the PSCs efficiency and would shine a light on the molecular design of the high performance nonfullerene acceptors.
New acceptor MeIC was synthesized through facile Knoevenagel condensation reaction between IT-CHO and the methylated CPTCN with a high yield of over $90\%$ (Scheme S1 in the Supporting Information). The immediate and the final product were fully characterized by nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time of flight mass spectrometry (MALDI-TOF-MS), and elemental analysis. MeIC shows good solubility in common organic solvents such as chloroform (CF), chlorobenzene (CB), and $o$ -dichlorobenzene (o-DCB).
Theoretical calculations were carried out using density functional theory (DFT) at the B3LYP/6-31G(d) level to evaluate the molecular energy levels of the two terminal groups and the acceptor (Figures S1 and S2 in the Supporting Information). MeIC shows similar dihedral angles to ITCPTC, which indicates that the introduction of methyl does not disturb the planarity of molecules. As expected, the LUMO level of MeIC is higher than that of the ITCPTC due to the introduction of week electron-donating methyl units. The energy levels of MeIC were also investigated by electrochemical cyclic voltammetry (CV) (Table 1; Figure S3, Supporting Information). The LUMO levels of MeIC and ITCPTC determined from the onset reduction potentials are $-3.92$ and $-3.96\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The experimental values are consistent with the calculated LUMO values. In order to deepen the understanding of the influence of methylation on molecular packing, the geometries of monomer and dimer are optimized at BLYP/def2-SVP level with resolution of identity approximation. The Grimme's D3 empirical dispersion correction was applied to accurately describe the weak interactions and basis set superposition error was corrected by $\mathsf{g C P}$ correction.[50,53,54] The binding energies of ITCPTC and MeIC do not have significant difference (0.007 eV), which indicates that the introduction of methyl does not alter the molecular packing due to its small steric hindrance (Table S1; Figure S4, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S5 in the Supporting Information, the acceptor molecules have four conformations. The conformation (c) shows the lowest energies relative to the other three conformations (a, b, and d) and could be the most stable. This may be attributed to the good planarity and S···O intramolecular noncovalent interactions.[19]
MeIC in CF solution $(10^{-5}~\mathrm{~M~})$ exhibits strong absorption from 520 to $740\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a maximum extinction coefficient of $2.11\times10^{5}~\mathrm{{\fontfamily{qpl}\selectfont M}\index{x^{-1}}}~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ at $674\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information), which is a slightly higher than that of ITCPTC $(2.04\times10^{5}~\mathrm{{\textmu}^{-1}~c m^{-1}}$ at $678\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). Figure 1b shows the film absorption spectra of ITCPTC, MeIC, and J71. Our previously reported wide-bandgap polymer donor J71 shows strong absorption at $400{\mathrm{-}}650\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , which is complementary to the absorption spectra of ITCPTC and MeIC acceptors. The thin film absorption of MeIC is similar to its solution one. It is worth mentioning that MeIC exhibits slightly broader absorption spectra at $500{-}700~\mathrm{nm}$ relative to ITCPTC in film, which may be due to the tighter molecular packing and better crystallite quality of MeIC. From grazing incident X-ray diffraction (GIXD) analysis of ITCPTC and MeIC in neat films (Figure 2), both acceptors show a preferred face-on orientation with $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking in out-of-plane direction. Compared with ITCPTC, MeIC exhibits much sharper $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and alkyl-to-alkyl stacking reflections, reduced $d$ -spacing $\mathbf{\bar{(MeIC)}}=\mathbf{3.50}\mathbf{\bar{A}}$ ; $\mathrm{ITCPTC}=3.60\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ and increased coherence length $\mathrm{(MeIC=}$ $2.54\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; $\mathrm{ITCPTC}=1.54~\mathrm{nm})$ of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, implying that the introduction of methyl could result in a tighter molecular packing and better crystallite quality. The scattering intensity and peak area also showed the improved crystallinity of MeIC. In in-plane direction, well-developed (100) peak was seen for MeIC with a packing distance of $1.90\ \mathrm{nm}$ and crystal coherence length of $7.56~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is tighter and larger than those in ITCPTC component. As a result, the electron mobility of MeIC is up to $8.4\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ with an average value of $7.9\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for ten devices, which is slightly higher than that of ITCPTC $(6.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ .
Photoluminescence (PL) quenching experiment was carried out to investigate the efficient exciton dissociation and charge transfer in BHJ blends (Figure 3). In comparison with the blend films before annealing, PL intensity of blend films after annealing displayed slightly high, indicating the reduced quenching degree after annealing. As can be seen in Figure 3, when excited at $550~\mathrm{nm}$ , BHJ thin-film emissions were highly quenched (by over $90\%$ ), indicating effective electron transfer from J71 to the acceptors. For the acceptor neat films, they show broad emission peak in the range of $710{-}860\ \mathrm{nm}$ when excited at a wavelength of $700~\mathrm{nm}$ . Obviously, the PL emission of the acceptors are effectively quenched after annealing (by
$95.2\%$ for ITCPTC and $97.5\%$ for MeIC) when using the photo excitation at $700\mathrm{nm}$ . The results provide sufficient evidence for effective hole transfer from the acceptors to polymer donor J71 even though the $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ difference between J71 and MeIC is only $0.17\ \mathrm{eV}.$
The photovoltaic properties of ITCPTC and MeIC were evaluated in a conventional device structure: ITO (indium tin oxide)/PEDOT:PSS (poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(sty rene-sulfonate))/J71:acceptors/PDINO (perylene diimide functionalized with amino N-oxide)/Al. The devices were optimized by changing the blend ratios of the polymer donor (D) to the acceptors (A) from 1.5:1 to 1:1.5 (See Table S2, Supporting Information). The optimal blend ratio of J71 and acceptors is 1:1. The PSCs based on J71/ITCPTC (1:1, w/w) exhibited a PCE of $10.37\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $17.23\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.897\mathrm{~V},$ and FF of $67.1\%$ . Compared with the ITCPTC-based device, MeIC-based device showed a higher $V_{O C}$ (0.926 V) due to its higher LUMO level and a similar $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ $(17.89\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ , demonstrating the success of our molecular design that the introduction of methyl substituents upshifts the LUMO level, thereby increasing $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Noticeably, the J71:MeIC-based device showed a PCE of $11.26\%$ with a FF of $67.89\%$ without any device post treatment, which is very favorable for low-cost industry-scale production of solar cells. To the best of our knowledge, $11.26\%$ is one of the highest PCE for the as-cast nonfullerene PSCs so far (Table S3, Supporting Information).
For further improving the efficiency of the devices, we use the thermal annealing treatment to optimize the active layer morphology. The optimized annealing condition is at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2~\mathrm{min}$ . In comparison with the devices without annealing, the PCEs were significantly improved mainly because of the increase of FF. As shown in Table 2 and the current density– voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves (Figure 4a), the FF of devices based on ITCPTC and MeIC are enhanced to 0.741 and 0.742, respectively. The J71:ITCPTC-based device showed a PCE of $11.63\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.896\mathrm{~V~}$ and a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $17.52\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Remarkably, for the device based on J71:MeIC, a high PCE of $12.54\%$ was achieved, with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.918\mathrm{~V~}$ and a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $18.41\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ Noticeably, the PCE of $12.54\%$ is among the highest PCEs for nonfullerene PSCs (Table S4, Supporting Information).
The external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves of the best devices are displayed in Figure 4b. For the two devices, the EQE values exceeded $70\%$ in the wavelength range of $500{-}740\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The EQE response of the J71:MeIC-based PSC is slightly stronger than that of the J71:ITCPTC-based device in the region of $550{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , due to the stronger light absorption of the J71:MeIC blended film (Figure 4b; Figure S4, Supporting Information). The calculated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values integrated from the EQE are 17.26, $17.96~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the ITCPTC- and MeICbased device, respectively, which is in good agreement with that measured from the $J{-}V$ curves.
We measured the current density at different light intensities to study the carrier recombination under short-circuit conditions (Figure 5a; Figure S8c, Supporting Information). Before annealing, both J71:ITCPTC and J71:MeIC-based devices show the same relationship of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto P^{0.97}$ . After annealing, both devices also displayed the same relationship of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto\:P^{1.0}$ . The higher slope after annealing implicated the weaker bimolecular recombination and the more effective carrier extraction in comparison with the devices before annealing. We further studied the trap-assisted recombination losses by plotting $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ as a function of light intensity. The relationship between $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and light intensity follows the relation: $V_{\mathrm{OC}}\propto\mathrm{In}P_{\mathrm{light}}$ (Figure 5b; Figure S7d, Supporting Information).[55] The $\bar{V}_{\mathrm{OC}}$ displayed a light intensity dependence with a slope 1.27 and $1.17k T/q$ for the device of ITCPTC and MeIC after annealing, respectively, implying that bimolecular recombination should be the dominate mechanism at open circuit. In comparison with the device before annealing, both of devices based on J71:ITCPTC and J71:MeIC gave higher slope $(1.39k T/q$ for ITCPTC and $1.27k T/q$ for MeIC). This indicates that the trap-assisted recombination is suppressed via annealing treatment. In addition, the photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ versus the effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ of the cells after anneling were measured to investigate the efficiencies of the exciton dissociation and charge collection (Figure 5c). $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is defined as $J_{\mathrm{L}^{-}}{J}_{\mathrm{D}}$ , where $J_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{D}}$ are the current densities under illumination and in the dark, respectively. $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is defined as $V_{0}{-}V,$ where $V_{0}$ is the voltage at which $J_{\mathrm{ph}}=0$ and $V$ is the applied voltage. The ratio of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ can reflect the exciton dissociation and charge collection efficiency, where $J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ represents the saturation photocurrent density. $J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ of the MeICbased cell is $19.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $V_{\mathrm{eff}}\geq2.0~\mathrm{V}$ , which is higher than that of the device based on J71:ITCPTC $(J_{\mathrm{sat}}=18.8~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}};$ ). Under short-circuit conditions, $P_{\mathrm{collection}}$ is $93.5\%$ for the J71:MeIC-based device, however the value for the J71:ITCPTCbased device is $91.8\%$ . As for the maximum power point, $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ of $82.9\%$ and $79.3\%$ were obtained for the device based on MeIC $(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=16.3\ \mathrm{mAcm^{-2}})$ and ITCPTC $(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=14.9\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2})$ , respectively. The larger value of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ for the J71:MeIC-based device implies the better charge extraction and collection, which contributes to the higher $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ .
In order to understand the effect of thermal annealing on the device performance, the charge-carrier mobilities were measured by space charge limited current (SCLC) method (Figure S8, Supporting Information; Table 2). The hole $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}})$ and electron $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}})$ mobilities of the J71:ITCPTC blend film are smaller than those of the J71:MeIC film. In addition, the $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{e}}$ apparently increase via annealing treatment, and the J71:MeIC film exhibited more banlanced $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}/\mu_{\mathrm{e}}$ (1.10) than that of J71:ITCPTC (1.34) after thermal annealing, which should be beneficial to the charge transport.
Thin film nanostructure of BHJ blend has significant impact on the charge transport and PSCs performance, which is thus characterized by various methods. GIXD was used to investigate the structure order of BHJ thin films before and after thermal annealing. As shown in Figure S9 in the Supporting Information, both thin films showed good molecular packing, and the crystalline content for both conditions is actually quite similar from GIXD results, which cannot be directly correlated with the device performance. The J71 diffraction signals were quite close to ITCPTC and MeIC both in (100) and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking region, therefore it is hard to clearly separate them out in the
BHJ blends. By fitting the high $q$ region $(0.95{-}2.2~\mathrm{A}^{-1})$ using two guassians, we noticed the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking peak for J71:MeIC is sharper, narrower, and centered in a slightly higher $\boldsymbol{q}$ position, implying that MeIC in blends is better packed. Resonant soft X-ray scattering (RSoXS) at $285~\mathrm{eV}$ was used to investigate the length scale of phase separation. As shown in Figure 6a, the thermal annealing led to obvious RSoXS intensity enhancement in the two BHJ thin films, indicating that the phase separation is better developed, due to material demixing upon the elevated temperature. In addition, upon thermal annealing, the broadened scattering profile and enhanced scattering intensity of the both devices indicated the improved domain purity, which is beneficial to reducing bimolecular recombination. Such structure features led to improved charge collection and extraction, which is favorable for increasing FF, thereby higher PCEs after annealing were obtained. Moreover, the morphology in MeICbased blends is quite different from ITCPTC-based blends (Figure 6a). The J71:ITCPTC blends showed a broad scattering hump centered at $0.0087\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ , giving a distance of $72~\mathrm{nm}$ . The RSoXS signal for J71:MeIC blend film is much weaker, which a less pronounced hump centered at $0.011\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ , giving a distance of $57\mathrm{nm}$ . The balance between length scale of phase separation and extent of phase separation in the BHJ blend films dictate the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the corresponding PSCs, and thus the higher values were obtained for the MeIC-based device.
Figure 6b showed surface and bulk morphology of BHJ thin films after thermal annealing, measured by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM). Both BHJ film surfaces were quite smooth with low root-meansquare (RMS) roughness. A better fibril type of morphology and bigger RMS surface roughness $(0.982\mathrm{nm})$ was seen for the J71:MeIC blends from AFM, which contributes to enlarge the contact between the active layers and the electrode. In the TEM images, the J71:ITCPTC blends showed a homogenized projection image with no clue of clear phase separation. This is due to the weak electron density contrast for polymer and acceptor material. In contrast, the J71:MeIC blends showed quite sharp TEM image with large area of flower type white domains of tens of nanometers. Yet a different surface and bulk morphology indicates that these white domains are only thin plates that buried inside BHJ thin film, and thus the electron mobility is not significantly larger than that in J71:ITCPTC blends.
In summary, a novel small molecule acceptor MeIC with a methylated end-capping group Me-CPTCN was developed. Compared to its unmethylated counterpart (ITCPTC), MeIC exhibits higher-lying LUMO level, stronger crystallinity, and absorption in the region of $520{-}740~\mathrm{nm}$ . The MeIC-based PSC with J71 as donor achieved a PCE of $11.26\%$ without any post treatment, which is very conducive to the production of industrial PSCs. Furthermore, the MeIC-based PSCs with thermal annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 min achieved very high PCEs of up to $12.54\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $18.41\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , significantly higher than those of the device based on J71 and nonmethylated ITCPTC $(11.63\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $17.52\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . We note that thermal annealing further optimized the phase separation size and purity. Though the enlarged phase separation after annealing caused tiny loss of excitons, it improved charge collection and extraction, suppressed the charge recombination, which contributed to obtaining high $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF, consequently resulted in high PCEs. As for the devices after annealing, the higher $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the PSC based on J71:MeIC can be attributed to more balanced $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}/\mu_{\mathrm{e}}$ , higher charge dissociation probability and charge collection efficiency, better molecular packing, and more suitable phase separation properties. These results revealed that the introducing of methyl substituents does not only raise the LUMO level value, but also significantly affect the molecular interactions and packing in thin film. Such a minor modification that leads to the significant improvement of the photo­voltaic performance is a very exciting discovery, indicative of new insights in the development of nonfullerene acceptors.
